"Los Angeles, USA", "role": "Major Scene"}, {"name": "London, UK", "role": "Cultural Hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: djent", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "djent"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2532560", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Djent"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Djent has reshaped the progressive metal genre and influenced music education, impacting thousands of musicians worldwide. Its legacy continues to resonate in contemporary music, with a dedicated following that spans several continents.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-30T01:01:16Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30504317321", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Musical genre, subgenre of progressive metal Created c. 1995.", "newValue": "Djent, a distinctive musical genre that emerged in the mid-1990s, represents a significant evolution within the progressive metal scene, characterized by its complex rhythmic structures and polyrhythmic grooves. Originating primarily in the global music landscape, djent is most commonly associated with bands such as Meshuggah and Periphery, whose innovative approaches to guitar playing and songwriting have redefined metal's sonic possibilities. This genre matters not only as a form of artistic expression but also as a cultural phenomenon that has influenced a generation of musicians and listeners alike, creating a vibrant community around its distinct sound.

The term "djent" itself was coined by guitarist Misha Mansoor of Periphery, who utilized it to describe the unique sound produced by heavily palm-muted guitar riffs. The genre gained traction through online platforms and social media, allowing artists to share their work and connect with fans globally. Key releases in the djent genre include Meshuggah's "ObZen" (2008) and Periphery's self-titled album (2010), both of which showcased complex time signatures and intricate guitar work, solidifying djent's place in the metal hierarchy. The advent of digital recording technology and widespread internet access played a crucial role in the genre's proliferation, enabling independent musicians to produce and distribute their music without traditional barriers.

